(English gist: tidy up the cards and the MCP commands. Workflow: (1) create a task — the task is formed and the developer is interviewed with clarifying questions; the task lands in the Todo column and carries its creation time and the user who created it. (2) Research — the task moves to in progress and shows in the Research column; then design, planning, implementation, each moving the task to the matching column. Wip branches are created, and committed to, at the implementation phase. When a phase starts, its file must already be created: new files like `research.phase`, `design.phase` that record when and by whom the phase was started and, once it ends, when it ended and how many tokens it cost.)

## Clarifications

- **Board layout?** Keep the current In progress column with its four staggered lanes (Research, Design, Planning, Implementation). Only the source of a card's phase changes: it comes from the `.phase` files. No new real columns.
- **Who writes the `.phase` files?** New MCP tools (`start_phase` / `finish_phase`, final names to be settled in design). The server stamps the time and the user itself; starting research moves `todo → in_progress`; starting implementation creates the git wip branch. `start_task` / `complete_task` stay for compatibility.
- **Where does the token count come from?** The server cannot see tokens. The calling agent passes the number to the finish-phase tool (optional field); the `begin_task` skill takes it from the phase sub-agent's reported total token usage.
- **What goes on a card?** All of: creator and creation time; the current phase with who started it and when; tokens (sum over finished phases); the branch chip as today.

## Problem statement

The delivery workflow (research → design → planning → implementation) is today only inferred from artifact file names and a single `start_task` that both starts the task and cuts its git branch, so the board cannot show who created a task, who started each phase and when, or what each phase cost, and branches appear before any code is written; the server must instead record task authorship and explicit per-phase `<phase>.phase` files through dedicated MCP tools, drive the board lanes from them, and create the wip branch only when implementation starts.

## Acceptance criteria

### Task authorship
- `create_task` stamps a new `created_by` field on the task record with the server-resolved user identity (the same identity rules as the current-task pointer: git `user.email`, falling back to the OS user). Existing tasks without it still load and display.
- `get_task` / `list_tasks` / the CLI expose `created_by`.

### Phase files and MCP tools
- Each of the four phases (`research`, `design`, `planning`, `implementation`) has an attached file `<phase>.phase` in the task directory, written only by the server.
- A new start-phase MCP tool creates `<phase>.phase` with the server-stamped start time and starting user. Unknown phase names are rejected.
- A new finish-phase MCP tool stamps the finish time on `<phase>.phase` and records an optional caller-supplied token count (non-negative integer).
- Starting the `research` phase on a `todo` task moves it to `in_progress` and points the caller's current-task pointer at it; under git branching it does **not** create a branch.
- Starting the `implementation` phase under git branching creates and checks out the task's wip branch (the branch behaviour `start_task` has today, including restart, vacate rule and rollback), unless the branch already exists. Without git branching it is record-only.
- Completing the task (`complete_task`) after implementation squashes/merges the wip branch exactly as today and finishes an still-open `implementation.phase`.
- Phase-ordering and re-entry rules (e.g. starting a phase twice, starting design before research is finished, reopening) are defined in the design and enforced with clear errors.
- `start_task` and `complete_task` keep working for callers that do not use phases (backwards compatible); `.phase` files are reserved names that `write_task_file` cannot overwrite.

### Board / cards
- The In progress column keeps its four lanes; a card's lane comes from its `.phase` files (the latest started phase). Tasks with no `.phase` files fall back to today's artifact-name derivation.
- A card shows: creator and creation time; the current phase with who started it and when; total tokens over finished phases (omitted when none); the branch chip as today.
- The task detail view shows the same information (at least each phase's start/finish/user/tokens).
- The UI stays read-only; handlers still never resolve the project.

### Workflow skills
- `begin_task` (both copies: `.claude/skills/begin_task/SKILL.md` and `plugins/mcp-task-manager/skills/begin_task/SKILL.md`) is updated: intake + clarifying questions + `create_task` leave the task in `todo`; each phase begins with the start-phase tool and ends with the finish-phase tool, passing the phase sub-agent's token total; implementation relies on the wip branch created at its start. Other skills that mention `start_task` (`implementation`, `workflow`) are aligned.

### Docs and quality gates
- `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md`, `internal/app/instructions.md` and tool descriptions describe the new field, files, tools and lane source.
- New behaviour is covered by tests (service, tools, git-branching fault injection for the moved branch creation, web view mapping); `go vet ./...` and `go test -race ./...` pass.

## Decisions after research

- **Subtasks have their own phases.** The phase tools and `.phase` files apply to subtasks as well as top-level tasks.
- **Keep the full history of phase runs.** When a phase output is rejected and the phase is redone, every run is kept (start/finish/user/tokens per run); nothing is overwritten.
- **Branches: no special effort.** If a task already has a wip branch when implementation starts, that is fine — reuse whatever existing `start_task` machinery does; no extra handling required for legacy/already-branched tasks.
- **User name format:** the existing sanitized identity (git email local part, e.g. `igor.cwer`, OS-user fallback) is acceptable for `created_by` and phase records.

## Decisions after design

- The design's open questions are accepted as proposed: phase/token data on in-progress cards only (detail view always); `update_task` to done leaves runs open (closable with `finish_phase`); no explicit skip-a-phase — trivial tasks use `start_task`/`complete_task`.
